    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The first great steppe empire of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eurasia">Eurasia</a>, the Xiongnu confederation dominated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a> from about 209 BCE.</li><li>For decades it forced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han China</a> to pay tribute in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> and princesses; its decimal army and court became the model for later steppe empires.</li><li>Han campaigns, natural disasters and rival <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chanyu">chanyu</a> split it in 48 CE; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-xiongnu">Northern Xiongnu</a> vanished as a power around 91 CE.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>In 200 BCE the Xiongnu trapped the Han founder, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-bang">Emperor Gaozu</a>, for seven days at Baideng; he escaped only after his envoys bribed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chanyu">chanyu</a>'s consort.</p></aside>
    </section>

